            <ArticleTitle>Self-management of urinary and fecal incontinence.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>38-45; quiz 47, 46</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/01.NAJ.0000441794.78032.f9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OVERVIEW" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Widely used by patients to control symptoms of chronic conditions such as diabetes, asthma, and arthritis, self-management can also help patients with urinary or fecal incontinence. The authors discuss the principles of self-management, the behaviors and skills self-managing patients need to acquire, and the nurse's role in reinforcing their use. They then describe strategies that can be incorporated within the framework of self-management to control urinary, fecal, or dual incontinence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
